  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nSaturday evening reveal weak ridging is in place from the southern \nplains through the central plains between a pair of shortwave \ntroughs over the central east coast and just offshore of the \nsouthern west coast. At the surface, modest high pressure centered \nover the Midwest is spread out through the southern and central \nplains, resulting in southerly winds across southwest KS. While \nthese winds will stay somewhat elevated overnight, clear skies \nshould help radiational cooling in allowing temperatures to drop \ninto the upper 30s/low 40s by sunrise Sunday morning. Daytime \nSunday, the aforementioned upper ridge and western shortwave trough \nwill dampen as they move eastward, yielding broad southwesterly mid-\nlevel flow across the central and southern Rockies. Weak lee \ncyclogenesis will occur in response over eastern CO, which will \nsupport 15-25 mph southerly winds gusting to 35 mph across the \ncentral plains. This along with 850-mb temperatures warming by 1-3 \ndegrees C will translate to afternoon highs ticking up a bit into \nthe upper 60s/low 70s. Sunday night, elevated southerly winds and \nclear skies will support Monday morning lows in the low to mid 40s.\n\nDaytime Monday, little to no change in the synoptic pattern is \nforecast by short range ensembles, as broad southwesterly flow \npersists across much of the western CONUS. Although, a powerful \ntrough will begin to impinge on the west coast by 06Z Tuesday. \nTherefore, nearly identical weather is expected on Monday with highs \nin the upper 60s/low 70s, the only difference being lighter \nsoutherly winds owing to the weak lee cyclone in eastern CO washing \nout.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe significant upper trough will make little progress eastward \nthrough Friday morning as a ridge amplifies downstream, but it \nfinally gains some eastward momentum daytime Friday and into the \nweekend. This synoptic evolution will yield a dry, warming trend \nthrough much of the business week, with afternoon highs increasing \nfrom the low 70s on Tuesday to the upper 70s/low 80s by Thursday, \nfollowed by a slight cooling trend into the weekend. Precipitation \nchances may also return associated with the potent wave, however \nconfidence remains low courtesy of predictability issues at that \nrange. For what it's worth, the NBM does have some reasonably \nwidespread slight chance (15-24%) pops with some low-end chance (25-\n54%) pops sprinkled in, but these should still be taken with a grain \nof salt.",
